## Understanding Micro-Frontends & Module Federation

- **Micro-Frontends (MFEs)** are a way to break down large frontend monoliths into smaller, more manageable, independently deployable applications.
- **Module Federation** is a Webpack feature that allows a JavaScript application to dynamically load code from another application at runtime.

> [!NOTE]
> Module Federation enables **runtime code sharing**, which is different from the traditional build-time sharing of NPM packages. This eliminates the need to republish host applications every time a shared micro-frontend is updated.

## Initial Project Setup

The video uses the `create-mf-app` scaffolding tool to quickly set up the project structure:
- `npx create-mf-app`
- This command is used to create two separate applications:
  - **Host App**: The application that will consume micro-frontends (running on port `8080`).
  - **Remote App**: The application that will expose micro-frontends (running on port `3000`).

## Exposing a Component from the Remote App

1.  A `Counter` component is created in the **remote** SolidJS application.
2.  To share this component, the `webpack.config.js` in the remote app is updated to include an `exposes` property in the `ModuleFederationPlugin` configuration.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> You must restart the Webpack development server after making changes to the Module Federation configuration for them to take effect.

```javascript
// In remote's webpack.config.js
new ModuleFederationPlugin({
  // ...
  name: "remote",
  filename: "remoteEntry.js",
  exposes: {
    "./Counter": "./src/Counter.jsx",
    "./CounterWrapper": "./src/CounterWrapper.jsx"
  },
  // ...
})
```

This generates a `remoteEntry.js` file, which is a manifest listing all the exposed modules.

## Consuming a Component in the Host App

1. The **host** app's `webpack.config.js` is configured to know where to find the remote MFE. This is done via the `remotes` property.

```javascript
// In host's webpack.config.js
new ModuleFederationPlugin({
  // ...
  remotes: {
    remote: "remote@http://localhost:3000/remoteEntry.js",
  },
  // ...
})
```
2. The component can then be imported dynamically and used just like a local component:

   `import Counter from "remote/Counter";`

## Cross-Framework Integration: SolidJS in React

To use a component from one framework (e.g., SolidJS) inside another (e.g., React), create a **framework-agnostic wrapper** in the remote app.

- This wrapper is a simple function that takes a DOM element as an argument and uses its own framework's rendering method to mount the component into that element.

```javascript
// remote/src/CounterWrapper.jsx
import { render } from "solid-js/web";
import Counter from "./Counter";

export default (el) => {
  render(() => <Counter />, el);
};
```

- In the React host app, use the `useRef` and `useEffect` hooks to get a reference to a DOM element and call the wrapper function to mount the MFE.

> [!TIP]
> This pattern is highly versatile. It allows you to integrate components from any framework (Vue, Svelte, SolidJS) into any other host framework (like React or Angular) by creating a simple mounting function.

```javascript
// react-host/src/App.jsx
import React, { useRef, useEffect } from "react";
import counterWrapper from "remote/CounterWrapper";

function App() {
  const divRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    counterWrapper(divRef.current);
  }, []); // Empty dependency array ensures it runs only once.

  return <div ref={divRef} />;
}
```
